index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..afa26106e81805a49769f30888d1d4a8bd9ae6d6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/apps/desktop/src/lib/voice-grok.md
@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+# Native Grok engine handoff
+
+The candidate production path uses a standalone UI-less plugin registered through
+`ctx.registerLiveVoiceProvider`. Core's `store/native-live-call.ts` owns the call,
+wake suspension, owner fencing and existing native controls; React views merely
+attach. This module remains the reviewed source/provenance reference and offline
+engine test subject. The sample below describes the engine-level contract, not
+permission for a composer to own its lifetime. See the candidate API section of
+`website/docs/developer-guide/desktop-plugin-sdk.md`.
+
+```ts
+const bridge = createGrokBridge({
+  owner,             // resolved connection/profile captured synchronously
+  request,           // captured gateway.request, not an ambient getter
+  rest,              // scoped hermesApi wrapper, maps body to its JSON option
+  subscribe,         // captured gateway event subscription
+  isBusy,            // authoritative session busy precheck; fail closed on unknown
+  isCurrent          // owner/session/transport identity check
+})
+const voice = new GrokLiveSession(bridge, {
+  onStatus, onError, onClosed,
+  onNotice, onTranscript, onSpeakingChange, onLevel
+})
+await voice.start({ sessionId, owner })
+voice.setMuted(true)
+voice.stopTurn()      // local output + xAI response.cancel only
+voice.checkOwner()    // call synchronously on owner/session/transport changes
+voice.end()           // no Hermes interruption/cancellation API is ever called
+```
+
+`GrokBridge` and `GrokFactories` can also be supplied directly. The engine imports no app stores, React, backend clients or plugin host. `status` is idle/connecting/listening/thinking/speaking/error. `onLevel` is microphone RMS (0..1), not remote playback RMS. Transcript timestamps are unknown (zero); only complete fragments are emitted.
+
+## Broker and mint contract
+
+Core's fixed `voice-grok-broker.ts` uses the existing authenticated native
+`hermesApiAs` door, with connection/profile captured before any await. It reads
+`GET /api/plugins/grok-voice-oauth/status` and, only on explicit call start,
+`POST /api/plugins/grok-voice-oauth/session`. The standalone provider receives
+only the narrow `GrokBridge`, never the broker plugin's identity or REST access.
+No backend patch, plugin REST expansion, OAuth refresh during readiness, or
+`/api/audio/grok-live/session` route is required.
+
+The mounted broker responds with `url`, `protocol_prefix`, `client_secret` and
+an inner `session`. Core pins the URL to
+`wss://api.x.ai/v1/realtime?model=grok-voice-latest`, validates the prefix, secret
+alphabet, numeric expiry (future, at most 330 seconds), session/tool types and
+24 kHz PCM input/output. It normalizes into the engine's contract:
+
+- `endpoint: 'wss://api.x.ai/v1/realtime'` (no query or arbitrary destination)
+- `subprotocol: 'xai-client-secret.'` (prefix, not an assembled credential)
+- `client_secret: { value, expires_at }` (Unix seconds)
+- `session_update: { type: 'session.update', session: { ...session, model: 'grok-voice-latest' } }`
+
+The broker's query model is preserved in `session.model`; only the ephemeral
+secret enters the WebSocket subprotocol. Core sanitizes broker errors and does
+not log or persist broker replies, URLs or session credentials. The existing
+broker returns `Cache-Control: no-store` on status, mint and error responses;
+the native IPC request type has no cache/header override and is not widened.
+Owner validity is checked before each broker request and after mint resolves.
+The current broker uses its own fixed voice/instructions/tools and ignores
+request configuration overrides.
+
+## Desktop selection versus server configuration
+
+On a modern backend advertising `providers`, that capability view remains
+authoritative (`capabilitySource: 'server'`, `providerSelection: 'server'`),
+and selection keeps the supported `voice.live_provider` server write.
+
+On the canonical legacy backend, the shared voice status still describes
+OpenAI only. Desktop composes that status with the fixed broker's separate
+readiness (`capabilitySource: 'desktop-broker'`,
+`providerSelection: 'desktop-owner'`). Historical top-level `available`, model,
+voice and reason remain OpenAI's; Grok readiness comes only from its own broker.
+This is configuration readiness, not proof of entitlement, quota, a minted
+session or an audio exchange. The registered `xai-oauth` adapter remains required
+by the existing single native menu; unavailable Grok never silently uses OpenAI
+or chained speech.
+
+Legacy provider choice is a device-local Desktop preference under
+`hermes.desktop.liveVoiceProviderByOwner.v1`, keyed by the captured
+`[connectionId, profile]`. Only enum values are persisted, never credentials or
+session identity. Choosing a full live engine writes the supported server mode
+`voice.voice_chat_mode` and persists the local provider after that write
+succeeds; provider-only selection writes no server config. It never sends
+`voice.live_provider` to that backend. Re-reading for a background owner and a
+renderer reload retain the same preference without editing the active owner.
+Modern server selection ignores this local compatibility preference.
+
+The preference joins `migrateTilesForProfile` (local rename only) and
+`dropTilesForProfile` (exact routed connection/profile). Storage remains
+best-effort via the app's standard persistence helpers; this local choice does
+not synchronize to another Desktop/device or other Hermes surfaces. An
+untagged legacy connection remains a distinct scope from an explicit registry
+connection; changing connection identity does not migrate its preference.
+
+## Receipt and lifetime rules
+
+The bridge submits `prompt.submit` with `queued: true`; it never steers, interrupts or resubmits. The engine first correlates the bounded canonical `admission_id` with the exact owner/session. The positive safe integer `user_row_id` / terminal `persisted_turn.user_row_id` remains the legacy fallback only when there is no canonical admission. Canonical terminal status/outcome must agree; errors and interruptions are not announced as success. Replays/foreign routes are ignored. Early completions are retained only during acceptance, capped at 16 events, each 3000 chars, TTL 10 s. The backend busy guard remains authoritative, while accepted tasks no longer block new admissions locally. Pending tasks and queued results share a capacity of 32, without evicting older work. At 120 s, only a local waiting notice is emitted: admitted tasks remain correlated until their terminal or hangup. An unconfirmed admission produces one ambiguous tool output, never automatic resubmission; a late ACK restores correlation without a second tool output. A completion that expired from the bounded pre-ACK buffer cannot be reconstructed here.
+
+A canonical queued receipt with a valid admission is correlatable without inventing a row, including an early terminal completion. A legacy queued receipt lacking both a row and an admission remains accepted/chat-only; no unrelated completion is voiced and no resubmission is attempted. Hanging up invalidates callbacks, unsubscribes and releases local audio/socket, but accepted work remains in Hermes/chat.
+
+## Audio and interruption limits
+
+PCM16 little-endian, mono, 24 kHz. Other AudioContext sample rates are refused. Capture uses the existing MIT AudioWorklet approach and native track muting. Bounds: input frame 24000 samples; decoded delta 128000 base64 chars; inbound socket message 256000 chars; outbound socket backlog 512000 bytes; playback backlog 30 s / 4096 waiting fragments, with at most 8 scheduled sources; pending tasks plus results 32; tool IDs 256 per call; interrupted response IDs and tracked cancellation IDs 64 each. Playback overflow interrupts local provider output, not the call or accepted Hermes work.
+
+Official xAI reference inspected: https://docs.x.ai/developers/rest-api-reference/inference/voice . It documents automatic interruption in server VAD, manual `response.cancel`, and `conversation.item.truncate`. VAD speech-start flushes local output and rejects interrupted response IDs. Manual stop sends only `response.cancel` to xAI. **No truncate is emitted:** this first engine does not track item-level audible playback offsets, so fabricating `audio_end_ms` would corrupt server history. The server may retain unplayed content. After any interruption, untagged audio deltas are rejected for the rest of the call: accepting them after a new response starts could replay an old response. Tagged late deltas are rejected by response ID. Speaking status follows response/delta events, not exact speaker-drain timing.
+
+A narrow compatibility exception leaves the call open for `invalid_request_error` / `response_cancel_not_active` only when its `event_id` matches our own tracked cancellation and the socket remains open. xAI documents recoverable errors but this exact code has not been confirmed publicly or live; this is a tested compatibility fixture, not production-provider acceptance. All unknown, auth and protocol errors remain fatal. An interrupted result reading can request another response from the same Grok context without reinjecting or resubmitting Hermes work; audible delivery remains unproven offline.
+
+The persistent core `NativeLiveControlsHost` renders one existing native `ConversationPill`, moving it into the captured target's visible composer or an active Settings/modal boundary, with a fallback after navigation. React draft adoption never reassigns the captured call to the ambient owner; only the runtime invalidates its owner/socket/session. Tests click mute/hangup on the actual runtime with a synthetic provider and prove modal focus ancestry. They are jsdom tests, not Chromium geometry/hit-testing acceptance.
+
+Core tests use isolated Vitest UI boundaries. Standalone adapter tests execute its built ESM and actual PCM implementation with synthetic browser/device boundaries. A separate opt-in core test accepts `GROK_NATIVE_PLUGIN_ARTIFACT` and registers that real artifact through the host context, exercising startup mute, detached delegation and plugin-unload teardown. None of these tests exercise a real microphone, OAuth, provider request, installation or deployment.
